<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke's Chair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Herzogstuhl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Duke's Chair is a double stone throne on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollfeld">Zollfeld</a> plain north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klagenfurt">Klagenfurt</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, probably assembled in the 9th century from reused Roman stones. After their installation at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princes-stone">Prince's Stone</a>, new dukes of Carinthia sat on it to receive homage and grant fiefs. The ceremonies lapsed in early modern times, and the chair is now a symbol of the province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dukes-chair.jpg" alt="Duke's Chair" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vojvodski_stol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
